package WstxContact {
    private import ScalarValues::*;
    doc /* Canonical contract of the standard FT8 contact (issue #10, CL-010-01..04, -06,
         -10..-14 in WstxRequirements): standard messages Tx1-Tx6, the server-owned
         auto-sequencing state machine, manual DX Call/Grid entry, Rx/Tx audio frequency
         selection including waterfall pointer actions, Rx Frequency pane routing,
         worked-before determination, the contact log and operator display items.
         Declared coverage is target; nothing here is an execution result.

         Ownership. WstxArchitecture::QsoAndLogging owns contact identity, the sequence
         state, next-message choice, completion and the per-profile log. It never keys
         PTT, opens audio or touches a radio. It consumes the TARGET #9 slice B consumer
         contract in WstxTransmit (TransmitArming, TransmitRequest, TransmitOutcome,
         HaltTransmit, SetTune) over the qso.transmit -> transmit.contacts connection of
         WstxArchitecture::StationProcess. #9 slice B owns the armed state, halt, PTT
         and pending-request replacement; TransmitService alone holds the station's
         explicit transmit authority (WstxArchitecture::RadioAuthority) and plans starts
         with WstxTransmit::PlanTransmitStart. #9 slice B is delivered (WstxTransmit
         transmit owner); the engine consumes it in-process, so a station with transmit
         capability and a current CL-022 grant can arm through Enable Tx.
         A receive-only station (no transmit capability) can still select rows, prepare
         contacts and log; every transmit action is then refused with
         transmit_unavailable. Wire contract: ContactWire below. The browser
         renders server-confirmed state and sends requests; it runs no sequence timer,
         modem or QSO state machine (#10 functional description).

         Behaviour reference: WSJT-X 3.3.0-beta5 source (C:/WSJT/wsjtx-3.3.0-beta5-src;
         WstxProductScope::BehaviorBaseline v3.3.0-beta4 is unchanged for these
         functions). Cited as MW = widgets/mainwindow.cpp, DMR =
         DecodedMessageReaction.cpp, DOP = DecodeOutputPlan.cpp, PL = widgets/plotter.cpp,
         CFG = Configuration.cpp, LQ = widgets/logqso.cpp, ADQ = logbook/AdifQso.cpp,
         WB = logbook/WorkedBefore.cpp, HT = HelpText.cpp. Operator decisions: issue #10
         comment 6539901776 (2026-10-31), items D1..D11. Deliberate differences are
         marked DIFFERENT with their reason.

         FT4 (#11 CL-011-02..04) reuses this contract unchanged except the rules marked
         FT4-NOTE: 7.5 s periods (WstxTransmit::Ft4Schedule), Tx4 always RR73 (MW
         ~11227 forces m_send_RR73 in FT4), ADIF MODE MFSK with SUBMODE FT4 (ADQ
         to_adif), Tx offset upper bound 2916 Hz (Ft4WaveformContract). Which mode a
         contact uses and what a mode change does: StationOperatingMode, ContactMode
         and ModeChange below (#11 slice B). */

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- messages
    enum def TxSlot { enum tx1; enum tx2; enum tx3; enum tx4; enum tx5; enum tx6; }
    enum def Tx4Form { enum rrr; enum rr73; }
    attribute def ReportRange {
        attribute minimumDb : Integer = -50;
        attribute maximumDb : Integer = 49;
        attribute manualDefaultDb : Integer = -15;
        doc /* Report value domain -50..+49 dB: DecodedText::report() clamps the decoded SNR
             (Decoder/decodedtext.cpp ~271) and widgets/mainwindow.ui rptSpinBox has
             min -50, max 49, value -15. Rendering is sign plus at least two digits,
             asprintf("%+2.2d") (MW genStdMsgs ~9738): -05, +03, +00, -15, +49.
             The value after selecting a decoded row is round-half-away-from-zero of its
             snr_db, clamped to this range; a row with null SNR, and manual DX entry,
             use the current report value, initially manualDefaultDb. */
    }
    attribute def StandardMessageSet {
        attribute dxCall : String;
        attribute myCall : String;
        attribute myGrid4 : String;
        attribute reportDb : Integer;
        attribute tx4 : Tx4Form;
        attribute tx1Text : String;
        attribute tx2Text : String;
        attribute tx3Text : String;
        attribute tx4Text : String;
        attribute tx5Text : String;
        attribute tx6Text : String;
        assert constraint reportInRange { reportDb >= -50 and reportDb <= 49 }
        doc /* Generated by the server only (MW genStdMsgs ~9690, genCQMsg ~9594), with
             R = the rendered report and single blanks:
             Tx1 "DX MYCALL GRID4"; Tx2 "DX MYCALL R" e.g. "K1ABC HB9BLA -12";
             Tx3 "DX MYCALL R" with R prefixed by "R" and no blank, "K1ABC HB9BLA R-12";
             Tx4 "DX MYCALL RRR" or "DX MYCALL RR73" per tx4; Tx5 "DX MYCALL 73";
             Tx6 "CQ MYCALL GRID4". MYCALL and GRID4 come from the selected profile's
             callsign and the first four characters of its locator
             (WstxProfiles::Settings); without a profile callsign, or a locator for
             Tx1/Tx6, no message set is generated and transmit stays unavailable with
             that reason. Without a DX call only Tx6 exists. Directed CQ (CQ DX/nnn)
             is not offered. Every generated text must pack unaltered with
             WstxTransmit::PackMessage (standard i3=1/2 family); a DX call that does
             not is refused at entry. Hashed/nonstandard <...> calls and contest
             forms belong to #24. Regenerating after a report, DX call or tx4 change
             replaces Tx2-Tx4 (and Tx1/Tx5 on a DX-call change); operator-edited text
             of a slot is kept until the next regeneration and requires server
             validation through PackMessage before it can be chosen as next.
             Tx4 default is rrr in FT8: m_send_RR73 starts false and is not saved
             (MW ~580, ~9661; Rr73Policy.cpp tx4AllowsRr73). FT4-NOTE: rr73, and
             always rr73 (MW send_rr73_for_tx4 ~9661 is true for FT4 whatever
             m_send_RR73; set_rr73_tx4 ~9676 forces it), see ToggleTx4Form. */
    }
    action def ToggleTx4Form {
        doc /* D7 adopted (MW on_txrb4_doubleClicked ~9047): switches tx4 between rrr
             and rr73 for the current contact and regenerates Tx4. Applies to the next
             transmission, never to one whose audio has started. FT4-NOTE: an FT4
             contact keeps rr73; set_tx4 rrr on it is refused out_of_range with field
             form and changes nothing (DIFFERENT from WSJT-X, which silently keeps RR73:
             a refusal never looks like an accepted change). */
    }
    action def OverrideReport {
        in requestedReportDb : Integer;
        doc /* D7 adopted (rptSpinBox, MW genStdMsgs): the operator sets the report sent
             in Tx2/Tx3; -50..+49 accepted, other values refused with the range. The
             override applies to the next transmission. A later row selection for a
             new partner sets the report from that row again (DMR appendReactedTail
             ~388-400). RST_SENT logs the report actually transmitted last. */
    }
    action def ToggleTx1Skip {
        doc /* D7 adopted (MW on_txrb1_doubleClicked ~9010 toggle_tx1_enabled_preference):
             with Tx1 skipped, a reply to a CQ or to a station not addressed to me starts
             with Tx2 and state report instead of Tx1/replying (DMR ~696-704), and
             choosing Tx1 as next selects Tx2 (MW ~8989). The Tx1 text is kept.
             Station-session preference, initially not skipped (mainwindow_settings
             tx1State default true); not part of the saved profile schema. */
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- contact
    enum def ContactOrigin { enum decodedRow; enum manualEntry; enum ownCq; }
    enum def SequenceStateName {
        enum idle; enum calling; enum replying; enum report; enum rogerReport;
        enum rogers; enum signoff; enum completed; enum cancelled;
    }
    attribute def Contact {
        attribute contactId : String;
        attribute origin : ContactOrigin;
        attribute dxCall : String[0..1];
        attribute dxGrid : String[0..1];
        attribute reportSentDb : Integer[0..1];
        attribute reportReceivedDb : Integer[0..1];
        attribute rxOffsetHz : Integer;
        attribute txOffsetHz : Integer;
        attribute holdTx : Boolean;
        attribute txParity : WstxTransmit::PeriodParity;
        attribute nextSlot : TxSlot;
        attribute lastTransmittedText : String[0..1];
        attribute sequenceState : SequenceStateName;
        attribute armState : WstxTransmit::ArmState;
        attribute mode : WstxOfflineDecode::Mode;
        attribute autoSequence : Boolean;
        attribute startUtc : String[0..1];
        attribute endUtc : String[0..1];
        attribute offeredForLog : Boolean;
        attribute saved : Boolean;
        doc /* Server-owned. contactId is a fresh lowercase UUID v4 created when a
             partner is prepared (row selection or manual entry) and kept through
             retries, reconnect, restart recovery and the log record
             (APP_WSTXRUST_CONTACT_ID). One active contact per station profile.
             A different partner while a contact is active (state replying..signoff)
             requires the explicit ChangeContact operation; a late row or stale request
             cannot change the transmitting partner. dxGrid is 4 or 6 Maidenhead
             characters or absent. nextSlot and lastTransmittedText are distinct and
             both shown. Reconnect loads this snapshot; it replays no arm, tune,
             next-message or transmit request. Another profile never inherits it.
             autoSequence is initially on (own choice, operator can switch it off;
             off means received messages never change nextSlot). armState is the
             read view of #9's TransmitArming, never armed by selection, entry, reconnect or startup (#10 CL-010-01,
             TransmitSafetyPolicy armedOnStartup=false). mode: ContactMode. */
    }
    calc def StationOperatingMode {
        in liveDecodingMode : WstxOfflineDecode::Mode[0..1];
        in selectionMode : WstxOfflineDecode::Mode;
        return mode : WstxOfflineDecode::Mode;
        doc /* #11 CL-011-02. The station's operating mode is the mode of whichever came
             last: the last started ft4|ft8 live decoding job (WstxOperatingReceive
             receive start, including the old Next receive mode control) or the last
             admitted operating selection (its requested mode, from admission on, even
             if the restart later fails); FT8 at a fresh boot. It persists across a
             receive stop and a spectrum-only job (review repair 2026-11-01: an FT4
             contact is not cancelled by stopping receive). An operating selection with
             action band keeps it: the band's entry is resolved in the station's
             operating mode, not in WireSelection's last mode (WSJT-X keeps the mode on
             a band change). FT4 is selected per station through the existing
             operating selection (action mode ft4, which retunes the dial to the FT4
             entry and restarts the live job in FT4) or the existing receive start;
             there is no second mode control, browser, rig-control, log or transmit
             authority for FT4: the #9 transmit panel's mode is a read-only display of
             this mode, its Tx offset input and the contact Tx input are bounded by the
             mode's domain (2916 Hz FT4, 2950 Hz FT8), both following station.tick. It
             sets the mode of a manually entered contact and of an own CQ, the Tx offset
             domain (FrequencyControls), station.tick period_ms (7500 FT4, 15000 FT8)
             and WstxTransmit::StationModeGate. */
    }
    attribute def ContactMode {
        doc /* #11 CL-011-03. A contact's mode is fixed when it is prepared: the selected
             row's mode (the live job that decoded it) or StationOperatingMode for
             manual entry and own CQ. All of its rules use that mode: messages
             (FT4-NOTE Tx4), TransmitSubmission requests (mode carried in every
             WstxTransmit::TransmitRequest, so #9 plans Ft4Schedule or Ft8Schedule and
             renders that waveform), period parity, TIME_ON/TIME_OFF period lengths, log
             MODE/SUBMODE and worked-before mode. Only decoded rows of the contact's
             mode count as received messages or for the 50 Hz rule; a row of the other
             mode never changes the sequence (cross-mode isolation). Re-preparing the
             same DX call in the other mode is a different contact (new contactId,
             ChangeContact rules). Each station process owns its own engine, transmit
             owner and contacts.adi, so simultaneous FT4 and FT8 stations share no
             contact, arm or log state. */
    }
    action def ModeChange {
        doc /* #11 CL-011-04. When StationOperatingMode differs from the mode of a contact
             in calling, replying, report, rogerReport, rogers or signoff, the engine
             (at its next step or command: POST /receive/start, /receive/stop and an
             admitted /receive/select run one synchronous contact step before their
             202 reply, otherwise at most one 50 ms step later) turns Enable
             Tx off through #9 (TransmitArming disarm, DisarmCause contactCancelled: no
             unkeyed request of the old mode remains) and cancels the contact as
             CancelContact (calling -> idle; replying..signoff -> cancelled, DX call and
             Tx1-Tx5 cleared, Tx6 next). A completed contact keeps its fields and its
             unsaved log review with its own mode; idle and cancelled contacts are
             unchanged. The Tx offset is clamped into the new mode's domain (an FT8
             2917..2950 Hz offset becomes 2916 Hz in FT4). Enable Tx afterwards starts a
             fresh own CQ in the new mode. DIFFERENT from WSJT-X, which keeps the DX
             call and Enable Tx across a mode change: a contact's messages, periods and
             log mode belong to one mode, so no contact or arm continues across modes.
             As WSJT-X (MW updateModeControlLock disables the mode controls while
             transmitting), an operating selection with action mode is refused busy,
             after the profile/revision checks and before any effect, while #9 reports
             PTT keyed, a transmission or Tune. Independently of the contact step, the
             #9 owner never keys or plays a request of another mode
             (WstxTransmit::StationModeGate), so no transmission of the old mode starts
             on the new mode's dial in the interval before the step; the contact's
             disarm cause is then refused (staleRequest) instead of contactCancelled. */
    }
    action def SelectDecodedRow {
        in rowIdentity : String;
        out prepared : Contact[0..1];
        doc /* CL-010-01. rowIdentity is the full validated profile/boot/source/job/
             generation/sequence tuple of a live result row with a concrete standard
             'from' call (WstxOfflineDecode::StandardFields). Effects, after server
             acknowledgement only (DMR handleDecodeSelection ~320-360, D-adopted):
             dxCall = the row's 'from' call; dxGrid = the row's grid exchange if any;
             report from the row SNR (ReportRange); rxOffsetHz = row frequency rounded
             to integer Hz; txOffsetHz = the same, unless Hold Tx is on or the row's
             'to' call is my call (replying to me keeps my Tx frequency, DMR ~320-333);
             txParity = opposite of the row's period parity; message set generated;
             nextSlot and state per FirstSlotOnSelection. Unresolved hashes, rows
             without a concrete call, old/wrong-profile tuples and non-standard
             families are refused with a reason. Never sets enableTx (DIFFERENT from
             CFG quick_call "double-click sets Tx enable": #10 safety rule). */
    }
    calc def FirstSlotOnSelection {
        in toIsMyCall : Boolean;
        in exchangeKind : String;
        in tx1Skipped : Boolean;
        return slot : TxSlot;
        doc /* DMR ~528-704. Row not addressed to me (CQ, or to another station): tx1
             (state replying), or tx2 (state report) when Tx1 is skipped. Row
             addressed to me: grid -> tx2/report; report -> tx3/rogerReport;
             R+report -> tx4/rogers; RRR or RR73 -> tx5/signoff; 73 -> tx5/signoff
             (offered for log once tx5 is sent). Responding to a station that answered
             my CQ is this case; CQ response mode (automatic choice of the responder)
             is #12 (D6), so a reply to my CQ always needs this explicit selection. */
    }
    action def EnterDxStation {
        in dxCallText : String;
        in dxGridText : String[0..1];
        out prepared : Contact[0..1];
        doc /* D1 adopted (MW dxCallEntry/dxGridEntry, on_dxCallEntry_returnPressed): call a
             station that was not decoded. The call is upper-cased; it must be a call
             WstxTransmit::PackMessage accepts as the 'to' call of the standard family
             (optionally /R or /P), else refused (no message set, no contact). Grid is
             optional, 4 or 6 Maidenhead characters (AA00..RR99[AA..XX]) else refused.
             Enter or Generate creates the contact with origin manualEntry, state
             replying, nextSlot tx1 (tx2/report when Tx1 skipped), the current report
             value, unchanged Rx/Tx offsets and the current Tx-period choice. Never
             sets enableTx. Same ChangeContact rule as selection while a contact is
             active. */
    }
    action def ChangeContact {
        doc /* Explicit server-accepted replacement of an active partner: the old
             contact is cancelled (or kept for review when already completed and
             unsaved) before the new one is prepared; Enable Tx is turned off. */
    }
    action def CancelContact {
        doc /* Abort the current contact: disarm (Enable Tx off), clear DX call/grid and
             Tx1-Tx5, select Tx6 (MW abortQSO ~9637). A transmission in progress
             continues unless Halt is also requested. A completed, unsaved contact
             stays available in the log review until saved or explicitly discarded. */
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- sequencing
    enum def ReceivedKind {
        enum grid; enum signalReport; enum rogerReport; enum rogers; enum seventyThree;
        enum otherFromPartner; enum partnerToOtherStation;
    }
    enum def SequenceEvent {
        enum prepareReply; enum startCq; enum receivedFromPartner; enum transmittedSignoff;
        enum cancel; enum halt; enum watchdogExpired; enum partnerWorksOther; enum none;
    }
    state def AutoSequence {
        attribute acceptedEvent : SequenceEvent;
        attribute received : ReceivedKind;
        attribute addressedToMe : Boolean;
        attribute fromPartner : Boolean;
        attribute autoSequenceOn : Boolean;
        attribute tx4IsRr73 : Boolean;
        attribute sentTx4 : Boolean;
        attribute firstSlot : TxSlot;
        attribute startedWithTx1Skipped : Boolean;
        entry; then idle;
        state idle; state calling; state replying; state report; state rogerReport;
        state rogers; state signoff; state completed; state cancelled;
        transition cq first idle if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::startCq then calling;
        transition prepareIdleTx1 first idle if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::prepareReply and firstSlot == TxSlot::tx1 then replying;
        transition prepareIdleTx2 first idle if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::prepareReply and firstSlot == TxSlot::tx2 then report;
        transition prepareIdleTx3 first idle if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::prepareReply and firstSlot == TxSlot::tx3 then rogerReport;
        transition prepareIdleTx4 first idle if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::prepareReply and firstSlot == TxSlot::tx4 then rogers;
        transition prepareIdleTx5 first idle if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::prepareReply and firstSlot == TxSlot::tx5 then signoff;
        transition prepareCqTx1 first calling if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::prepareReply and firstSlot == TxSlot::tx1 then replying;
        transition prepareCqTx2 first calling if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::prepareReply and firstSlot == TxSlot::tx2 then report;
        transition prepareCqTx3 first calling if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::prepareReply and firstSlot == TxSlot::tx3 then rogerReport;
        transition prepareCqTx4 first calling if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::prepareReply and firstSlot == TxSlot::tx4 then rogers;
        transition prepareCqTx5 first calling if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::prepareReply and firstSlot == TxSlot::tx5 then signoff;
        transition gridToReport first replying if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::receivedFromPartner and autoSequenceOn and addressedToMe and fromPartner and received == ReceivedKind::grid then report;
        transition reportFromReplying first replying if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::receivedFromPartner and autoSequenceOn and addressedToMe and fromPartner and received == ReceivedKind::signalReport then rogerReport;
        transition reportFromReport first report if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::receivedFromPartner and autoSequenceOn and addressedToMe and fromPartner and received == ReceivedKind::signalReport then rogerReport;
        transition rogerReportFromReplying first replying if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::receivedFromPartner and autoSequenceOn and addressedToMe and fromPartner and received == ReceivedKind::rogerReport then rogers;
        transition rogerReportFromReport first report if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::receivedFromPartner and autoSequenceOn and addressedToMe and fromPartner and received == ReceivedKind::rogerReport then rogers;
        transition rogerReportRepeat first rogerReport if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::receivedFromPartner and autoSequenceOn and addressedToMe and fromPartner and received == ReceivedKind::rogerReport then rogers;
        transition rogersToSignoff first rogerReport if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::receivedFromPartner and autoSequenceOn and addressedToMe and fromPartner and received == ReceivedKind::rogers then signoff;
        transition rogersRepeat first signoff if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::receivedFromPartner and autoSequenceOn and addressedToMe and fromPartner and received == ReceivedKind::rogers then signoff;
        transition reportBackFromRogers first rogers if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::receivedFromPartner and autoSequenceOn and addressedToMe and fromPartner and received == ReceivedKind::signalReport then rogerReport;
        transition seventyThreeInRogers first rogers if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::receivedFromPartner and autoSequenceOn and addressedToMe and fromPartner and received == ReceivedKind::seventyThree then completed;
        transition sentRr73 first rogers if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::transmittedSignoff and sentTx4 and tx4IsRr73 then completed;
        transition sent73 first signoff if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::transmittedSignoff then completed;
        transition otherStationWhileReplying first replying if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::partnerWorksOther and autoSequenceOn then replying;
        transition otherStationWhileReport first report if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::partnerWorksOther and autoSequenceOn and startedWithTx1Skipped then report;
        transition cancelCalling first calling if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::cancel or acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::halt or acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::watchdogExpired then idle;
        transition cancelReplying first replying if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::cancel then cancelled;
        transition cancelReport first report if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::cancel then cancelled;
        transition cancelRogerReport first rogerReport if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::cancel then cancelled;
        transition cancelRogers first rogers if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::cancel then cancelled;
        transition cancelSignoff first signoff if acceptedEvent == SequenceEvent::cancel then cancelled;
        doc /* Transition table (DMR planProcessMessage ~528-655, planAutoSequence ~724;
             MW guiUpdate ~8056-8091). prepareReply is SelectDecodedRow or EnterDxStation;
             firstSlot is the FirstSlotOnSelection result (tx1 replying, tx2 report incl.
             a Tx1-skip start, tx3 rogerReport, tx4 rogers, tx5 signoff). A selection
             while a contact is active first passes ChangeContact (cancel, then idle).
             Next message per state: calling tx6, replying tx1,
             report tx2, rogerReport tx3, rogers tx4, signoff tx5; completed and
             cancelled select tx6 with Enable Tx off.
             Received messages count only when: Auto Seq on, a standard message whose
             'to' is my profile callsign and 'from' the contact's DX call, decoded in the
             partner's period, at any audio frequency (DMR ~724). Messages of other
             stations never change the sequence (DMR ~517-524) but stay visible in the
             Rx Frequency pane. Classification: grid exchange -> grid; report without
             R -> signalReport; R+report -> rogerReport; RRR or RR73 -> rogers; 73 ->
             seventyThree. One decoded row (same validated identity tuple) counts once,
             however often it is delivered or re-read; a re-decode of the same period
             yielding the same partner message also counts once per period.
             Repeats: no retry counter or timeout (WSJT-X has none for FT8, DMR/MW
             ~8844 fast modes only); the next message is sent again each of my periods
             while Enable Tx is on until a counted message changes the state; the Tx
             watchdog (TxWatchdog) bounds unattended repetition. A repeated earlier
             partner message maps back as WSJT-X does: a signalReport in rogers returns
             to rogerReport (resend Tx3); R+report in rogerReport/rogers keeps rogers
             (resend Tx4); rogers in signoff resends Tx5. Messages not in the table
             (grid in report.., 73 in signoff, anything after completed/cancelled)
             change nothing.
             Completion: transmittedSignoff is TransmitService's confirmed completed
             transmission of a Tx4 RR73 or Tx5 73 of this contact (MW message_is_73
             ~492, m_sentFirst73). On completion Enable Tx turns off (CFG
             disable_TX_on_73 default true ~3746) and the contact is offered for log
             once. Receiving 73 while in rogers (after Tx4 RRR) completes without
             sending more (DMR ~601-616). DIFFERENT: WSJT-X then leaves Enable Tx on
             with Tx6 selected; here Enable Tx turns off because Auto Seq never
             starts another contact (#10).
             partnerWorksOther (DMR planAutoSequence ~775-791, MW ~7427
             auto_sequence(decodedtext,25,50); 50 Hz rule): in replying, or in report
             when the contact started with Tx1 skipped, with Auto Seq on, the DX call's
             decoded message to another call within 50 Hz inclusive of my txOffsetHz
             disarms and halts a transmission already started; the state is kept. #9
             has no dedicated DisarmCause for it: the disarm is recorded with cause
             operator (supervisor decision on #10 review, 2026-10-31).
             halt and watchdogExpired turn Enable Tx off and keep the state (the
             contact can resume after an explicit Enable Tx), except in calling where
             the CQ ends. cancel follows CancelContact. Esc is halt plus cancel. */
    }
    attribute def TransmitSubmission {
        attribute contactId : String;
        attribute slot : TxSlot;
        doc /* How QsoAndLogging uses WstxTransmit::TransmitRequest. While armed it keeps
             one pending request for the next period of the contact's txParity with the
             current nextSlot text, txOffsetHz and mode, and a new requestSequence for
             every change. A sequence, text, report or offset change replaces the pending
             request through #9 until its audio starts; #9 then refuses audioStarted and
             the change applies to the following period (Now buttons are not adopted,
             D7). lastTransmittedText changes only on a started outcome; the contact
             reaches completed only on a completed Tx4 RR73/Tx5 73 outcome; refused or
             failed outcomes disarm with the visible refusal. A client timeout, colour
             change or disconnected press never means RF stopped. Tx offset domain FT8
             200..2950 Hz (Ft8WaveformContract tone 0; FT4-NOTE 200..2916). */
    }
    action def SetEnableTx {
        in enable : Boolean;
        doc /* Separate explicit operator action (Alt+N where the browser delivers it,
             HT Alt+N). Maps to WstxTransmit::TransmitArming (armed/disarmed is #9
             state). On: transmissions of the next slot start in the contact's parity
             periods through TransmitSubmission; with no partner, next Tx6 means
             calling CQ (state calling). Off (disarm): no new request; a transmission
             already started finishes (WSJT-X Enable Tx off lets the current Tx finish). */
    }
    action def HaltTx {
        doc /* Halt Tx button, Alt+H (HT) and Esc. Sends WstxTransmit::HaltTransmit
             (POST /transmit/halt), stopping a transmission or Tune now and disarming
             (MW on_stopTxButton_clicked). Esc additionally cancels the active contact
             as MW keyPressEvent ~3683 (abortQSO); D5: Esc acts on every page state and
             in every focused field and closes no panel. With no transmit capability
             (receive-only station) Esc only cancels the contact and sends no halt.
             Shown as halted only after #9 confirms; until then pending/unknown. The
             keyboard halt is sent by the always-loaded page script (never waiting for
             the contact client), skipped only when the station is known receive-only;
             the Esc cancellation follows the answered halt. Esc on a completed contact
             only selects Tx6 and keeps its fields (Log QSO still works). */
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- watchdogs
    attribute def TxWatchdog {
        attribute limitMinutes : Integer = 6;
        attribute minimumMinutes : Integer = 1;
        attribute maximumMinutes : Integer = 99;
        attribute remainingMinutes : Integer;
        assert constraint limitDomain { limitMinutes >= minimumMinutes and limitMinutes <= maximumMinutes }
        doc /* D4 adopted (CFG TxWatchdog default 6 ~3749; MW tx_watchdog ~14646,
             update_watchdog_label ~14680). Server-owned station-session setting,
             default 6 at each station start, settable 1..99 minutes. DIFFERENT: WSJT-X
             0 = disabled is not offered; a safety bound always applies. It counts only
             while Enable Tx is on (counter 0 when off) in whole minutes and resets on
             operator activity: any accepted operating request of this station from
             the browser or MCP (select, entry, message/slot choice, frequency, report,
             Enable Tx, Auto Seq, Log), and the start of a transmission whose text
             differs from the previous one (MW ~8139). Mouse movement the server cannot
             observe is not activity. At expiry the server sends #9's halt (the
             transmission stops, as MW ~7690/7763), turns Enable Tx off, keeps the
             contact state, and shows "Tx watchdog expired" until the next Enable Tx.
             The remaining minutes are shown while Enable Tx is on (WD:nm). */
    }
    attribute def TuneWatchdog {
        attribute limitSeconds : Integer = 90;
        attribute minimumSeconds : Integer = 1;
        attribute maximumSeconds : Integer = 300;
        assert constraint limitDomain { limitSeconds >= minimumSeconds and limitSeconds <= maximumSeconds }
        doc /* D4 adopted (CFG TuneWatchdog default true, TuneWatchdogTime default 90 s
             ~3750-3751; mainwindow_slots.cpp ~590; MW stopTuneATU ~12728). Always
             enabled (DIFFERENT: not switchable off), 1..300 s, default 90 s per
             station session. Starts when #9 confirms Tune on; at expiry the server
             ends Tune through #9; the remaining seconds are shown on the Tune control. */
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- frequencies
    enum def PointerAction {
        enum click; enum shiftClick; enum rightClick; enum ctrlClick;
        enum leftDoubleClick; enum rightDoubleClick;
    }
    calc def PointerEffect {
        in pointerAction : PointerAction;
        in pointerHz : Integer;
        doc /* D3 adopted (PL mouseReleaseEvent ~976, mouseDoubleClickEvent ~1012; MW
             setFreq4 ~12816). pointerAction and pointerHz: pointerHz is the waterfall/spectrum frequency under the
             pointer from the fixed 0..3000 Hz axis, rounded to integer Hz (the native/
             projected readout of WstxRecordingReceive is unchanged). click -> Rx;
             shiftClick or rightClick -> Tx; ctrlClick -> Rx and Tx; leftDoubleClick
             -> Rx, then the Re-decode of the last completed interval (D3 "decode
             there"; the fixed full-band decoder of D8 has no narrow decode, results
             within the Rx window then route to the Rx Frequency pane); rightDoubleClick
             -> Rx and Tx. Explicit Tx pointer actions move Tx even with Hold Tx on, as
             WSJT-X setFreq4 (Hold Tx only stops implicit moves on selection).
             A Tx outside its domain is refused with the domain; Rx accepts 0..3000.
             Each is a server request; markers move only after confirmation, the
             pointer readout is local. Touch: tap = click; Tx/both use the labelled
             numeric inputs. No pointer action enables Tx. */
    }
    attribute def FrequencyControls {
        attribute rxOffsetHz : Integer;
        attribute txOffsetHz : Integer;
        attribute holdTx : Boolean;
        assert constraint rxDomain { rxOffsetHz >= 0 and rxOffsetHz <= 3000 }
        assert constraint txDomain { txOffsetHz >= 200 and txOffsetHz <= 2950 }
        doc /* Labelled numeric Rx Hz and Tx Hz inputs (MW RxFreqSpinBox/TxFreqSpinBox),
             Hold Tx (requested vs confirmed shown separately). Tx<-Rx/Rx<-Tx copy
             buttons are not adopted (agent choice 2026-10-31: ctrl-click and the
             numeric inputs cover them); F11/F12 steps are not adopted (D7). FT4-NOTE Tx max 2916. */
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- Rx pane
    calc def RoutesToRxFrequencyPane {
        in rowFrequencyHz : Real;
        in rxOffsetHz : Integer;
        in firstCallIsMyCall : Boolean;
        in containsMyCallAndDxCall : Boolean;
        return routed : Boolean = firstCallIsMyCall or containsMyCallAndDxCall or
            (rowFrequencyHz >= rxOffsetHz - 10.0 and rowFrequencyHz <= rxOffsetHz + 10.0);
        doc /* D2 adopted (DOP decideRouting ~391-441): a decoded row is shown in the Rx
             Frequency pane when its audio frequency is within +/-10 Hz inclusive of the
             confirmed Rx offset (FT8 and FT4 alike), or its first ('to') call equals
             the profile callsign exactly, or it contains my call followed by the DX
             call. It also stays in Band Activity. The station's own transmitted
             messages appear in the pane labelled Tx with their period UTC (CFG
             Tx2QSO default true; MW displayTransmittedText ~8151). The pane
             accumulates rows across periods in the browser from results it already
             receives (no extra request), newest last, at most 500 rows (own bound),
             with the same unread/resume-follow behaviour as Band Activity; it clears
             on profile change, mode/band change and Erase (local only). An Rx change
             affects only later routing. Selecting a pane row is SelectDecodedRow. */
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- log
    attribute def LogRecord {
        attribute contactId : String;
        attribute call : String;
        attribute gridsquare : String[0..1];
        attribute mode : String;
        attribute submode : String[0..1];
        attribute rstSent : String[0..1];
        attribute rstRcvd : String[0..1];
        attribute qsoDate : String;
        attribute timeOn : String;
        attribute qsoDateOff : String;
        attribute timeOff : String;
        attribute band : String[0..1];
        attribute freqMHz : String[0..1];
        attribute stationCallsign : String;
        attribute myGridsquare : String[0..1];
        attribute txPwrW : String[0..1];
        attribute commentText : String[0..1];
        doc /* ADIF 3.1.8 ADI fields (field set follows ADQ to_adif ~50-75 and LQ, D10):
             CALL, GRIDSQUARE (only when known; DIFFERENT from WSJT-X writing it empty),
             MODE FT8 (FT4-NOTE MODE MFSK, SUBMODE FT4), RST_SENT = report of the last
             transmitted Tx2/Tx3 (MW ~8105-8127), RST_RCVD = the partner's last counted
             report, both rendered as in ReportRange; QSO_DATE/QSO_DATE_OFF YYYYMMDD,
             TIME_ON/TIME_OFF HHMMSS UTC; BAND from the confirmed dial frequency per the
             ADIF band enumeration (e.g. 20m), absent when the dial is not confirmed;
             FREQ in MHz with six decimals = confirmed dial + Tx offset (LQ ~384,
             mainwindow_slots.cpp ~545), absent when unconfirmed; STATION_CALLSIGN and
             MY_GRIDSQUARE from the profile; TX_PWR watts (operator entry, decimal >0,
             optional, D10); COMMENT single line, printable ASCII without '<' and '>', at
             most 1024 characters (optional, D10); a COMMENT containing '<' or '>' is
             refused beside the field (DIFFERENT from WSJT-X to_adi_text stripping them:
             a reviewed record is never altered); APP_WSTXRUST_CONTACT_ID = contactId.
             Name, operator, propagation, satellite and contest exchange fields are not
             adopted (D10). Retain checkboxes: power and comments may be retained for
             the next review within the station session.
             Start time (MW set_dateTimeQSO ~8944): the UTC start of the period of this
             contact's first transmitted Tx2, or one period before its first
             transmitted Tx3; otherwise the start of its first transmitted message's
             period. End time: the UTC end of the last period in which a counted
             message of the contact was transmitted or received. Both server-owned;
             the browser never substitutes its clock. */
    }
    enum def LogOutcome { enum saved; enum alreadySaved; enum invalidField; enum conflict; enum storageFailed; }
    action def OfferLogReview {
        doc /* D10. Automatic: once per contactId when it completes (AutoSequence
             completed), the in-page review opens prefilled (WSJT-X prompt on the first
             sent 73/RR73, MW guiUpdate ~8056; auto-accept without review is not
             adopted, matching WSJT-X's default AutoLog ContestingOnly behaviour, CFG
             ~3731-3734, LQ initLogQSO ~359-369). Manual Log QSO (button, Alt+Q, HT):
             available whenever a contact has a DX call, in any state, using the same
             contactId and the times observed so far. The operator may correct call,
             grid, reports, times, power and comments; invalid fields are refused beside
             the field. The review never hides Halt Tx. Offers queue per contactId,
             oldest first, at most 8 unsaved: a completed or manually offered contact
             stays offered until saved or discarded (discard_review), even after
             CancelContact or ChangeContact; a later contact's offer waits behind it
             (the same contactId replaces its own offer); a full queue refuses Log QSO
             with state_conflict and leaves the completed contact available for a later
             Log QSO; while such a completed, unqueued, unsaved contact exists, preparing
             another contact (select_row, enter_dx) or starting a CQ is refused with
             state_conflict, so it is never dropped. ContactSnapshot review is the oldest
             unsaved offer. */
    }
    action def SaveContact {
        in reviewed : LogRecord;
        out outcome : LogOutcome;
        doc /* Exactly once per contactId per profile. Storage: the selected profile
             directory's file contacts.adi (ADIF 3.1.8 ADI with header), written as a
             complete new file in the same directory, flushed and atomically replaced,
             as WstxProfiles::SaveSelectedProfile; saved is acknowledged only after the
             replace succeeds. A repeated save of the same contactId with identical
             fields returns alreadySaved and adds nothing; different fields after a
             save return conflict (history is read-only in this slice). Failure leaves
             the review visibly unsaved with retry under the same contactId. A new
             contact with a call already in the log is a separate contact (no dupe
             refusal; WSJT-X LogBook::add appends without a dupe check) and the review
             shows its worked-before status. Restart recovers completed unsaved
             contacts of the current boot only as unsaved; nothing is auto-saved. */
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- worked before
    enum def WorkedStatus { enum notWorked; enum workedOtherBandOrMode; enum workedThisBandAndMode; enum unknown; }
    action def WorkedBeforeQuery {
        in calls : String[0..64];
        in band : String;
        in mode : String;
        out statuses : WorkedStatus[0..64];
        doc /* D9 adopted (WB indexes calls keyed by band+mode and band; LogBook::match).
             Determined by the server from the profile's contacts.adi including contacts
             saved this session: workedThisBandAndMode when a record has the same CALL,
             BAND and MODE/SUBMODE; workedOtherBandOrMode when only CALL matches; else
             notWorked. Calls compare case-insensitively on the full call. DXCC entity,
             grid, zone and LoTW highlighting are not adopted (need cty.dat, B-list of
             #25). The browser sends at most one query per published period with at most
             64 distinct concrete calls of that period's rows; rows show unknown (no
             indicator) until answered and for hashed/absent calls. Indication uses a
             text/icon label plus colour; it changes no decoding, sequence or log. */
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- displays
    attribute def OperatorDisplays {
        attribute utcClockText : String;
        attribute periodElapsedSeconds : Integer;
        attribute periodSeconds : Integer = 15;
        attribute transmitting : Boolean;
        attribute rxLevelDb : Integer[0..1];
        assert constraint progressBounds { periodElapsedSeconds >= 0 and periodElapsedSeconds < periodSeconds }
        assert constraint levelBounds { rxLevelDb == null or (rxLevelDb >= 0 and rxLevelDb <= 90) }
        doc /* D11 adopted. UTC clock HH:MM:SS (MW labUTC) from the server's UTC
             observation in the status stream, advanced locally between observations
             for display only; marked stale with the link; never a scheduling input.
             Tx progress (MW ~8388): elapsed whole seconds of the current period over
             the period, "v/15" (FT4-NOTE 7.5), with a Transmitting label (and colour)
             while #9 reports an active transmission. Rx level meter (widgets/
             signalmeter.cpp, meterwidget.cpp): 0..90 dB, rxLevelDb = round(10 log10(mean(s^2))) with s
             the canonical receive samples scaled to 16-bit full scale (32768), over the
             last status interval, at most one value per second on the status link;
             labelled "high" above 85 dB and "low" below 15 dB (meterwidget.cpp ~61/~64 thresholds)
             in text and colour; null shows unavailable when audio is not running. */
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- ADI log
    attribute def AdiLimits {
        attribute importBytesMax : Integer = 67108864;
        attribute importRecordsMax : Integer = 100000;
        attribute fieldCharactersMax : Integer = 1024;
        attribute fieldNameCharactersMax : Integer = 64;
        attribute importChunkBytesMax : Integer = 16384;
        attribute historyPageRecordsMax : Integer = 10;
        attribute historyPageBytesMax : Integer = 16384;
        doc /* Application admission limits (#10 text; not ADIF limits). Exactly at a
             limit is admitted, one over is refused and publishes no record of that
             import. Inside a record only white space is permitted between a field value
             and the next tag; any other text there is length_mismatch (an inexact LEN is
             never silently truncated). The stored log is not limited by one import's record count. */
    }
    enum def AdiError {
        enum malformed; enum lengthMismatch; enum unsupportedCharacter; enum fieldTooLong;
        enum tooManyRecords; enum tooLarge; enum missingRequiredField; enum invalidValue;
        enum identityConflict; enum integrityMismatch;
    }
    action def ImportAdi {
        in importId : String;
        in totalBytes : Integer;
        in sha256 : String;
        out newCount : Integer;
        out duplicateCount : Integer;
        out conflictCount : Integer;
        out error : AdiError[0..1];
        doc /* ADIF 3.1.8 ADI only (header optional, ended by <EOH>; records end <EOR>;
             tags case-insensitive <NAME:LEN[:TYPE]> with exact LEN in characters; only
             white space between fields within a record, see AdiLimits). Characters: printable ASCII 0x20-0x7E, plus
             TAB/CR/LF only inside unknown-field values; anything else
             unsupportedCharacter. Required per record: CALL, QSO_DATE (valid
             YYYYMMDD), TIME_ON (HHMM or HHMMSS), MODE; supported fields that are present
             must be valid (LogRecord domains; FREQ and TX_PWR positive decimals,
             GRIDSQUARE 2/4/6/8 Maidenhead) else invalidValue with record index and
             field name. Unknown fields (including other programs' APP_ fields and
             USERDEF fields) are preserved verbatim, name and value, in the stored record
             and re-exported unchanged after the supported fields, as WSJT-X
             logbook/ADIF tools keep unknown tags; they are never interpreted or
             executed. Header fields are not stored.
             Identity: a valid APP_WSTXRUST_CONTACT_ID UUID is the record identity.
             Without it the natural key is (CALL case-folded, QSO_DATE, TIME_ON to the
             minute, BAND or, without BAND, FREQ's ADIF band, MODE, SUBMODE); a new such
             record receives a fresh contact id, written as APP_WSTXRUST_CONTACT_ID.
             Duplicate: same identity (or, without id, same natural key) as a stored or
             earlier record of the same file and identical supported-field values;
             skipped and counted. Conflict: same identity with any differing supported
             field value; counted, reported with record index and the first differing
             field. Any conflict or error fails the whole import: zero records are
             published (validation completes before publication); counts are still
             reported. Otherwise new records are appended in file order in one atomic
             replace of contacts.adi.
             Retry identity: importId is a caller-chosen UUID. Reserving the same
             importId with identical totalBytes and sha256 returns the existing import
             resource or its final result without a second effect; different values are
             state_conflict. sha256 of the complete received bytes is checked before
             validation (integrityMismatch). One import per profile at a time; the
             resource is retained until the next reservation or restart. Chunks follow
             the WstxRecordingReceive import pattern: exact Content-Range, at most
             importChunkBytesMax each, in order. Bulk transfer yields to status/control
             and never delays HaltTransmit. */
    }
    action def ExportAdi {
        doc /* GET /contacts/export.adi streams the whole profile log as ADIF 3.1.8 ADI:
             header with ADIF_VER 3.1.8, PROGRAMID WSTXRUST and CREATED_TIMESTAMP, then
             every stored record in storage order with its supported fields (LogRecord
             set), APP_WSTXRUST_CONTACT_ID and preserved unknown fields verbatim. Content
             type application/octet-stream with attachment filename contacts.adi; no
             content coding; streamed with backpressure behind status/control. Export
             then import of the same file yields only duplicates. */
    }
    action def ContactHistory {
        in cursor : Integer;
        in limit : Integer;
        doc /* GET /contacts?cursor=&limit= returns exactly
             {v:1,profile_id,total,cursor,next,more,records}; records are the stored
             records in append (storage) order with index cursor..; storage is
             append-only in this slice so indexes are stable. limit 1..10 (default 10),
             cursor 0..total (total returns an empty page), page at most 16384 encoded
             bytes. Each record is exactly {index,contact_id,call,gridsquare,mode,
             submode,rst_sent,rst_rcvd,qso_date,time_on,qso_date_off,time_off,band,freq,
             station_callsign,my_gridsquare,tx_pwr,comment,unknown_field_count} with
             absent values null, rendered literally. Read-only inspection; no edit or
             delete. */
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- wire contract
    enum def ContactRefusal {
        enum invalid_request; enum unsupported_version; enum unauthorized; enum forbidden_origin;
        enum wrong_profile; enum not_found; enum request_too_large; enum unsupported_media_type;
        enum state_conflict; enum stale_revision; enum busy; enum counter_exhausted;
        enum contact_active; enum no_contact; enum row_unavailable; enum row_not_standard;
        enum invalid_call; enum invalid_grid; enum invalid_report; enum invalid_message;
        enum out_of_range; enum profile_incomplete; enum transmit_unavailable;
        enum redecode_unavailable; enum log_invalid_field; enum log_conflict;
        enum import_failed; enum storage_unavailable; enum snapshot_limit;
    }
    enum def ContactAction {
        enum select_row; enum enter_dx; enum cancel; enum choose_next; enum edit_message;
        enum set_report; enum set_tx1_skip; enum set_tx4; enum set_frequency; enum set_hold_tx;
        enum set_parity; enum set_auto_seq; enum set_watchdogs; enum enable_tx; enum tune;
        enum redecode; enum open_log; enum discard_review;
    }
    item def ContactCommand :> WstxArchitecture::StationRequest {
        attribute v : Integer = 1;
        attribute profile_id : String;
        attribute boot_id : String;
        attribute command_id : String;
        attribute contact_revision : Integer;
        attribute commandAction : ContactAction;
        doc /* POST /contact/command, body at most 1024 UTF-8 bytes, exact keys
             {v,profile_id,boot_id,command_id,contact_revision,action} (commandAction is the JSON key action) plus exactly the
             parameters of its action (all required, nullable where stated):
             select_row {row:{source_id,job_id,generation,sequence},replace};
             enter_dx {dx_call,dx_grid|null,replace}; cancel {};
             choose_next {slot:"tx1".."tx6"}; edit_message {slot,text};
             set_report {report}; set_tx1_skip {enabled}; set_tx4 {form:"rrr"|"rr73"};
             set_frequency {rx_hz|null,tx_hz|null} (null = unchanged; pointer actions use
             this with the PointerEffect result); set_hold_tx {enabled};
             set_parity {parity:"even"|"odd"}; set_auto_seq {enabled};
             set_watchdogs {tx_minutes,tune_seconds}; enable_tx {enabled,
             safety_revision|null} (safety_revision: the CL-022 safety revision shown in
             the snapshot transmit view; for enabled true a value different from the
             station's current revision is stale_revision with field safety_revision);
             tune {enabled}; redecode {} (last completed interval of the current live
             job); open_log {} (manual Log QSO); discard_review {contact_id}.
             replace true is the explicit ChangeContact; false with an active contact is
             contact_active. command_id is a caller UUID: the server retains the last 16
             command receipts per boot; an identical body with a retained command_id
             returns 200 with the same receipt and no effect; a different body with it
             is state_conflict. contact_revision must equal the current revision
             (stale_revision otherwise), except cancel, enable_tx false and tune false,
             which are admitted with any earlier revision. Validation order is the
             WstxOperatingReceive S2 order (route, Origin, TLS/session, size/media/syntax,
             version/enums/UUIDs/safe integers, profile, boot/revision, capability,
             state). Transmit actions on a station without transmit capability, or
             without a current CL-022 grant (a plaintext profile), are
             transmit_unavailable; Enable Tx arms #9 with the confirmed CL-022 safety
             revision it carries (#9 itself refuses a changed revision, reported
             stale_revision with field safety_revision; no check-then-arm race). A transmit outcome refused without a #9 disarm
             (duplicateSequence) disarms with cause refused and is never resubmitted
             unchanged; the engine's own disarms are not shown as refusals. */
    }
    item def CommandReceipt :> WstxArchitecture::StationOutcome {
        attribute v : Integer = 1;
        attribute command_id : String;
        attribute contact_revision : Integer;
        attribute accepted : Boolean = true;
        doc /* 202 exactly {v:1,profile_id,boot_id,command_id,contact_revision,accepted:true},
             at most 384 bytes: accepted, not yet confirmed effect. Refusals use the
             existing envelope {error:{code,field}} with code a ContactRefusal and field
             the offending JSON key or null; nothing is changed by a refusal. */
    }
    item def ContactSnapshot :> WstxArchitecture::StationOutcome {
        attribute v : Integer = 1;
        attribute contact_revision : Integer;
        attribute snapshot_sequence : Integer;
        doc /* GET /contact returns 200 exactly
             {v:1,profile_id,boot_id,contact_revision,snapshot_sequence,monotonic_ms,
             utc_ms,transmit,settings,contact,review,watchdog}, at most 4096 encoded
             bytes (503 snapshot_limit otherwise, never truncated). transmit is #9's read
             view exactly {capability:"unsupported"|"unavailable"|"available",
             arm_state,last_disarm_cause,tune,pending_sequence,last_text,
             last_period_utc,halt_state,safety_revision,refusal} (safety_revision the
             current CL-022 safety revision of the selected radio scope or null);
             settings exactly {auto_seq,tx1_skip,
             tx_minutes,tune_seconds,rx_hz,tx_hz,hold_tx,parity,report}; contact null or
             exactly {id,origin,dx_call,dx_grid,report_sent,report_rcvd,state,next_slot,
             tx4,messages,start_utc,end_utc,offered_for_log,saved} (report_sent the
             report of the last transmitted Tx2/Tx3 rendered, null until one is sent)
             with messages an array
             of six strings or nulls (Tx1..Tx6, each at most 37 characters); review null
             or exactly {contact_id,record,outcome,error} with record the LogRecord keys
             and outcome pending|saved|unsaved; watchdog exactly {tx_remaining_minutes,
             tx_expired,tune_remaining_seconds}. contact_revision advances on every
             accepted command and every server-side sequence/transmit/watchdog change;
             snapshot_sequence also on clock-free material changes. No content coding:
             /contact, /contacts and /contacts/* are not in the six-route
             WstxOperatingReceive::ExactCompressedGetRoute allowlist and stay identity. */
    }
    item def ContactStateEvent {
        attribute v : Integer = 1;
        attribute 'type' : String = "contact.state";
        doc /* New versioned WebSocket event on the existing wstx.v1 socket, exactly
             {v:1,type:"contact.state",data:{profile_id,boot_id,contact_revision}}, at
             most 192 bytes, sent when contact_revision changes, coalesced to at most one
             per second per peer and always sent within 1 s of a change; the browser then
             GETs /contact (one pending GET per browser). It is not a StatusReply:
             station.status stays exactly {v,type,id,data{version,radio}}. Control and
             status keep priority over it as over optional visualization. */
    }
    item def StationTickEvent {
        attribute v : Integer = 1;
        attribute 'type' : String = "station.tick";
        doc /* New versioned WebSocket event, exactly
             {v:1,type:"station.tick",data:{profile_id,boot_id,utc_ms,period_ms,
             transmitting,rx_level_db}}, at most 224 bytes (the exact keys with two UUIDs
             and a 13-digit utc_ms already need 220; corrected from 192 by the #10
             implementation, review required). Rate per peer by its display
             subscription: Detailed 1 per second; Thin and Off 1 per 5 s; plus at most one
             extra event per transmit start/stop. A peer that never chose a display
             subscription (a bare status observer) receives neither event. The browser interpolates the UTC clock
             and Tx progress locally from utc_ms with its monotonic clock between ticks
             (display only); rx_level_db shown is the latest tick value. Budget: Off idle
             with two clients adds at most 2 x (224 + 2 WS + 29 TLS + 160 accounted
             TCP/ACK) / 5 s = 166 B/s per direction, so the status-only idle total stays
             within WstxStationProtocol::ThinLinkBudget 1000 B/s; Thin stays within the
             existing 3000 B/s live envelope. TC-007-005 measures both. */
    }
    attribute def ContactWire {
        doc /* Routes of WstxContact (selected-profile mode only; environment-only mode
             404 not_found): GET /contact; POST /contact/command; POST /contact/worked
             (WorkedBeforeQuery: exactly {v,profile_id,boot_id,band,mode,calls}, calls 0..64
             strings of at most 13 characters, body at most 2048 bytes; 200 exactly
             {v:1,band,mode,statuses:[{call,status}]}, at most 4096 bytes; read-only, at
             most one per published period per browser); POST /contacts (SaveContact:
             exactly {v,profile_id,boot_id,command_id,record}, body at most 8192 bytes so
             that a 1024-character comment fits with worst-case JSON escaping;
             201 {v:1,contact_id,outcome:"saved"}, 200 alreadySaved, 409 log_conflict,
             400 log_invalid_field with field, 503 storage_unavailable leaving the review
             unsaved); GET /contacts (ContactHistory); GET /contacts/export.adi
             (ExportAdi); POST /contacts/imports {v,profile_id,boot_id,import_id,
             total_bytes,sha256} 201/200 reserve; PUT /contacts/imports/{id} chunks;
             GET /contacts/imports/{id} exactly {v:1,id,state,received_bytes,total_bytes,
             new,duplicate,conflict,error} with error null or {code,record,field}
             (ImportAdi); POST /transmit/halt (WstxTransmit::HaltTransmit).
             All use the existing HTTP/TLS authentication, exact Origin on mutations,
             JSON media rules and error envelope; the 1024-byte body cap applies except
             the stated 2048-byte worked-before body, 8192-byte save body and 16384-byte
             import chunks. Mutations are never
             replayed automatically; reconnect reads GET /contact first. MCP uses the
             same station operations. */
    }

    dependency contactOwner from Contact to WstxArchitecture::QsoAndLogging;
    dependency transmitBoundary from TransmitSubmission to WstxTransmit::TransmitRequest;
    dependency transmitOwner from TransmitSubmission to WstxArchitecture::TransmitService;
    dependency contactPromise from AutoSequence to WstxRequirements::'FR-007';
    dependency transmitPromise from TxWatchdog to WstxRequirements::'FR-008';
    dependency logPromise from LogRecord to WstxRequirements::'FR-011';
}
